1
00:00:00,176 --> 00:00:04,220
Now, if you are using an AI agent on a

2
00:00:04,285 --> 00:00:07,270
system, as I'm doing it here on a VPS,

3
00:00:07,992 --> 00:00:11,796
sometimes permissions can be a problem. And

4
00:00:11,988 --> 00:00:14,315
the main takeaway, just to get that out of

5
00:00:14,315 --> 00:00:15,679
the way, is that

6
00:00:16,305 --> 00:00:20,093
there isn't really a secure way around this

7
00:00:20,093 --> 00:00:22,805
problem. If you want to give your agent more

8
00:00:22,821 --> 00:00:24,988
permissions, you have to live with the fact

9
00:00:25,068 --> 00:00:27,361
that more things can go wrong.

10
00:00:28,179 --> 00:00:29,797
Or if you don't want to give it the

11
00:00:29,797 --> 00:00:31,960
permissions, you have to live with the fact

12
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,685
that it can't do as much stuff. You have to

13
00:00:34,685 --> 00:00:37,297
choose one of these 2 things essentially.

14
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:40,341
With that in mind though, what you can do

15
00:00:41,319 --> 00:00:42,313
outside of the agent

16
00:00:42,921 --> 00:00:46,511
is you can edit your VPS user, or you can

17
00:00:46,527 --> 00:00:49,171
create a new one for the agent and edit it

18
00:00:49,171 --> 00:00:52,589
such that it can get sudo access for all

19
00:00:52,798 --> 00:00:54,622
commands or certain commands

20
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:58,074
without being asked for the password, with

21
00:00:58,268 --> 00:01:00,559
all the negative consequences this has, of

22
00:01:00,623 --> 00:01:00,833
course.

23
00:01:01,430 --> 00:01:01,951
What you can do

24
00:01:02,475 --> 00:01:04,589
is you can run a command called

25
00:01:05,179 --> 00:01:05,523
visudo,

26
00:01:07,580 --> 00:01:10,099
with sudo in front of it since this requires

27
00:01:10,359 --> 00:01:11,367
elevated privilege,

28
00:01:13,086 --> 00:01:14,366
-f /etc/sudoers,

29
00:01:20,364 --> 00:01:22,779
This will open up a text editor again.

30
00:01:23,363 --> 00:01:25,406
And in there, you can now enter your

31
00:01:25,406 --> 00:01:25,893
username,

32
00:01:26,706 --> 00:01:29,127
then all=all:all

33
00:01:30,265 --> 00:01:30,619
like this,

34
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:36,193
nopassword:all.

35
00:01:37,514 --> 00:01:39,190
What this means essentially is that for the

36
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:41,668
user Max, we won't ask for a password for

37
00:01:41,896 --> 00:01:42,825
all commands.

38
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:46,601
If you wanted to limit this to certain

39
00:01:46,601 --> 00:01:49,148
commands instead of typing all here,

40
00:01:49,841 --> 00:01:52,598
you can instead just list the specific

41
00:01:52,647 --> 00:01:53,957
commands here that

42
00:01:54,604 --> 00:01:56,787
it should be allowed to execute without a

43
00:01:56,787 --> 00:01:57,321
password.

44
00:01:58,001 --> 00:01:59,602
For that, however, you need to include the

45
00:01:59,602 --> 00:02:02,333
full path to where that tool is installed.

46
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:03,845
For example,

47
00:02:04,376 --> 00:02:08,432
/usr/bin/systemctl like this,

48
00:02:09,206 --> 00:02:11,760
And then even say something like restart. So

49
00:02:11,776 --> 00:02:14,811
that would allow to run the restart command

50
00:02:14,843 --> 00:02:17,059
without a password, but no other commands.

51
00:02:17,316 --> 00:02:19,051
So you could be more restrictive here.

52
00:02:19,902 --> 00:02:22,857
Here for demo purposes, I'll use all instead

53
00:02:23,756 --> 00:02:27,033
and then hit Ctrl+X and Y to save and hit

54
00:02:27,113 --> 00:02:27,370
Enter.

55
00:02:28,414 --> 00:02:29,345
And now if I run

56
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:32,348
sudo ls/,

57
00:02:33,216 --> 00:02:35,448
I can run this without entering a password.

58
00:02:35,802 --> 00:02:38,864
Again, this has disadvantages. Obviously, if

59
00:02:38,864 --> 00:02:42,005
someone gets access to this user, they can

60
00:02:42,118 --> 00:02:45,147
perform tasks as a root user without being

61
00:02:45,163 --> 00:02:46,782
the root user, without being asked for a

62
00:02:46,782 --> 00:02:48,882
password. Now, since we locked down the

63
00:02:48,882 --> 00:02:49,635
server quite a bit,

64
00:02:50,373 --> 00:02:54,172
we are more secure, but of course not 100%

65
00:02:54,172 --> 00:02:55,999
secure. So that can be an issue. In

66
00:02:55,999 --> 00:02:58,804
addition, the AI of course now can also do

67
00:02:58,804 --> 00:03:00,888
all kinds of stuff. Nonetheless, to show you

68
00:03:00,888 --> 00:03:03,463
what you can do now, if I now Again, start

69
00:03:03,463 --> 00:03:04,779
the PyCoding agent.

70
00:03:05,656 --> 00:03:06,110
I can

71
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:09,505
grab that URL to this Docker article, or

72
00:03:09,895 --> 00:03:11,778
I'll just copy the content as Markdown.

73
00:03:13,126 --> 00:03:15,628
And then I could give this AI a task like,

74
00:03:17,174 --> 00:03:18,315
please install Docker

75
00:03:19,729 --> 00:03:20,902
as explained in this article.

76
00:03:21,753 --> 00:03:24,196
And then here I like to do these XML tags.

77
00:03:24,244 --> 00:03:26,406
That is optional though, not required. I'll

78
00:03:26,406 --> 00:03:27,941
paste my Markdown content here.

79
00:03:29,657 --> 00:03:32,437
And then again, I'll say follow these

80
00:03:32,790 --> 00:03:34,413
installation instructions

81
00:03:35,216 --> 00:03:36,148
step by step.

82
00:03:36,807 --> 00:03:39,089
And it should now be able to install Docker

83
00:03:39,603 --> 00:03:39,828
as

84
00:03:40,808 --> 00:03:43,282
instructed in the official docs because it

85
00:03:43,298 --> 00:03:45,065
has those sudo permissions. So it will be

86
00:03:45,146 --> 00:03:46,897
able to execute sudo commands

87
00:03:47,733 --> 00:03:49,789
without being prompted for a password, which

88
00:03:50,046 --> 00:03:51,155
naturally would fail.

89
00:03:52,376 --> 00:03:53,131
So now it

90
00:03:53,661 --> 00:03:54,497
gets to work here.

91
00:03:55,188 --> 00:03:57,183
Already executed a sudo command here

92
00:03:57,922 --> 00:03:59,113
and it's done.

93
00:04:00,013 --> 00:04:02,072
And that can of course be

94
00:04:02,667 --> 00:04:05,514
convenient with all the disadvantages I

95
00:04:05,514 --> 00:04:06,335
mentioned. But still

96
00:04:06,865 --> 00:04:10,677
having an AI agent on a VPS can be useful.

97
00:04:11,128 --> 00:04:12,977
If you wanna use it, of course it's your

98
00:04:13,058 --> 00:04:13,926
personal decision.
